so first of all we all lead really busy  lives we have to go to work we see our  friends we go outside all the time but  actually during the pandemic now more  than ever we stay at home a large part  of our day we have to work at home we  have to study at home we see friends  through skype at home so our home  environment is becoming more and more  our  main environment  therefore  our home is becoming super important in  our lives and we kind of want to create  a space that can inspire us that can  reduce stress and help us reach our  goals  so in this episode i want to talk a  little bit about how our environment can  help reduce stress or can cause stress  and which environmental factors you have  to think about when you want to redesign  your home such that you can create an  optimal  man case to reach your study or perhaps  your working goals  so grab yourself a coffee  and let's have coffee time  so when we think of our environment we  may consider only the things that we can  see but there are actually two other  senses that are really important and  that's what we can hear and what we can  smell so i will go over all three of  these senses  and talk a little bit about how we can  optimize them to increase this perfect  zen-like atmosphere  so let's first dive a little bit into  stress  so our stress response is actually  regulated by the hypothalamic pituitary  adrenal axis or hpa for short  and this includes our hypothalamus  our pituitary gland which is this  pre-like gland under our thalamus and  lastly our adrenal gland  and when we are in a stressful situation  this access then ensures that we go from  a sustenance state of relaxing and  digestion towards a stressful state or a  stress response which is an increased  heart rate or increased blood pressure  and if this stressful state is  maintained for a long time this can  actually lead to heart failure or stroke  or other  adverse bodily  issues as well as mental issues and  usually this stress respond is triggered  by external factors such as a bear  approaching but sometimes they can also  be triggered by involuntary  internal or external  factors such as for example when you  enter a really messy room and you think  about oh [ __ ] i have to clean this  and i don't have the time to do this  when am i gonna do this then our stress  response is also triggered our blood  level rises and if this continues for a  long time then this leads to adverse  health effects so let's first talk a  little bit about our visual environment  and how this can be used to increase or  decrease our stress levels  so most of us are nowadays staring at  our screen all day but our visual field  is actually a little bit larger and this  is to ensure that we're always alert for  certain attacks from bears for example  that could enter our environment at any  time  but it is also shown that having  visual clutter around you can be  monitored even if you're not looking at  it  so visual clutter is everything in your  visual environment  and this can increase your stress  response so doing a merry condo here and  there is maybe not a bad idea so this is  then one of the factors that can  increase our stress response but there  are also certain things that can  decrease our stress response and one of  these things has been shown to be nature  or having plans around us for example  this researcher roger ulrich  showed that patients that were in their  hospital room that overlooked a growth  of trees actually had a better recovery  trajectory than the patients that  overlooked a brick wall  so this shows that nature can really  reduce our stress response and increase  our recovery  and another thing why  researchers think that nature is so good  is this so-called attention restoration  theory  so nature allows us to have this soft  focus or soft fascination as they call  it  and this is that nature grabs our  fascination without us having to  consciously put effort for it  and because this is kind of an  unconscious process of fascination our  attention resources can then  increase again for when we have to put  hard attention on something so there are  then two factors that nature can help us  with and one of them is our stress  response to decrease this and the other  is attention restoration which can go up  with nature so having a lot of plants  around you and this urban green jungle  is maybe not only super aesthetic but  also super good for your mental  well-being as well so another important  factor is actually light in our visual  environment so light is one of the most  important things that controls our  circadian rhythm and our circadian  rhythm  is the rhythm that tells us when we have  to go to bed when we are sleepy and when  it's time to wake up and our circadian  rhythm is  reset by the amount of sunlight we  receive during the day so it's really  important to get enough sunlight during  the day even if you're at home  most of the day and therefore i would  recommend for example to put your  working desk next to a window or  somewhere where there's enough light  or to go outside if that's still  possible  and another thing to consider is to keep  light out during the night so not to  have this blue light from your screen  for example or even the light from the  moon can disturb our sleeping rhythms so  think about this when you're creating  your nighttime environment as well  so then for the visual environment we  have two things to consider one is to  have nature around us at all times and  the other is to have a good light source  during the day the second environmental  factor that i want to talk about is the  things we can hear  so it has been shown by research that  bad noise such as construction noise or  your neighbor screaming all day can  increase our stress response even if  it's subconscious  so investing in a pair of really good  noise cancelling headphones in my  opinion is really important if you live  in the city such as i do or if you don't  have money for that just getting a pair  of really good earplugs could also do  the job and for a positive reduction of  stress  and increase of relaxation we can think  of music therapy so there has been a lot  of research into music therapy but  it is really individualized what people  like in their music so for example for  some people rock music  may be really relaxing whereas other  people like to listen to more classical  music  but in general it has been shown that if  people listen to the music they prefer  their psychological as well as  physiological  response to stress goes down  so it is a good idea to just invest in a  nice speaker and put on your favorite  music if you want to have a little  relaxing  moment during the day  and lastly i want to talk about a little  bit of forgotten sense and that is smell  so the olfactory system is the system  that records what we smell in our  environment  and bad smells such as urine or  something rotten has been shown to  increase our stress response  so just cleaning your space is already  pretty important but there's also a lot  of  research into  smells that could help us reduce our  stress response but the thing is with  smell it's one of the senses that's  extremely correlated to emotional memory  so what people find smelling  nicely is really individualized to their  memory and their experience  so for example if you really like the  smell of sunflowers  because it reminds you of this one trip  you took to france where you were  cycling through the sunflower fields  for another person this may remind them  of their stuffy aunts that used to spray  perfume everywhere  that smelled of sunflowers so for them  it may be a triggering smell whereas for  you it's a really relaxing smell  however there are some common  stress decreasing smells has been shown  by dr yoshinori  and these smells  include  lavender the aroma of coffee beans  cypress which is this really popular  tree in japan and lastly pine  so if you don't have any  emotional memories or negative emotional  memories with these smells you could  think of including them in your smell  environment  by for example  aromatherapy  or  perfume  so this was a little deep dive into the  research  on environmental factors that could  increase or decrease our stress response  and i hope when you think of redesigning  your environment you will think a little  bit about how to include some visual  factors such as nature to increase this  soft fascination and having a good light  source  some sound factors so playing some nice  piano music on the background  and lastly not to forget smell as well  so some fresh coffee grinds around or  some flowers it's always really nice and  then i think you will have this ultimate  perfect zen cave to produce all your  work  during these tough corona times  so thank you for joining me on coffee  time and hope to see you next time bye  [Music]  you
 

